## THE BARRISTER’S DREAM

@style verse
  They sought it with thimbles, they sought it with care;
  [aki 2.5zw]They pursued it with forks and hope;
  They threatened its life with a railway-share;
  [aki 2.5zw]They charmed it with smiles and soap.

@style verse
  But the Barrister, weary of proving in vain
  [aki 2.5zw]That the Beaver’s lace-making was wrong,
  Fell asleep, and in dreams saw the creature quite plain
  [aki 2.5zw]That his fancy had dwelt on so long.

@style verse
  He dreamed that he stood in a shadowy Court,
  [aki 2.5zw]Where the Snark, with a glass in its eye,
  Dressed in gown, bands, and wig, was defending a pig
  [aki 2.5zw]On the charge of deserting its sty.

@style verse
  The Witnesses proved, without error or flaw,
  [aki 2.5zw]That the sty was deserted when found:
  And the Judge kept explaining the state of the law
  [aki 2.5zw]In a soft under-current of sound.

@style verse
  The indictment had never been clearly expressed,
  [aki 2.5zw]And it seemed that the Snark had begun,
  And had spoken three hours, before any one guessed
  [aki 2.5zw]What the pig was supposed to have done.

@style verse
  The Jury had each formed a different view
  [aki 2.5zw](Long before the indictment was read),
  And they all spoke at once, so that none of them knew
  [aki 2.5zw]One word that the others had said.

@style verse
  “You must know—” said the Judge: but the Snark exclaimed “Fudge!”
  [aki 3zw]That statute is obsolete quite!
  Let me tell you, my friends, the whole question depends
  [aki 2.5zw]On an ancient manorial right.

@style verse
  “In the matter of Treason the pig would appear
  [aki 2.5zw]To have aided, but scarcely abetted:
  While the charge of Insolvency fails, it is clear,
  [aki 2.5zw]If you grant the plea ‘never indebted.’

@style verse
  “The fact of Desertion I will not dispute;
  [aki 2.5zw]But its guilt, as I trust, is removed
  (So far as related to the costs of this suit)
  [aki 2.5zw]By the Alibi which has been proved.

@style verse
  “My poor client’s fate now depends on your votes.”
  [aki 3zw]Here the speaker sat down in his place,
  And directed the Judge to refer to his notes
  [aki 2.5zw]And briefly to sum up the case.

@style verse
  But the Judge said he never had summed up before;
  [aki 2.5zw]So the Snark undertook it instead,
  And summed it so well that it came to far more
  [aki 2.5zw]Than the Witnesses ever had said!

@style verse
  When the verdict was called for, the Jury declined,
  [aki 2.5zw]As the word was so puzzling to spell;
  But they ventured to hope that the Snark wouldn’t mind
  [aki 2.5zw]Undertaking that duty as well.

@style verse
  So the Snark found the verdict, although, as it owned,
  [aki 2.5zw]It was spent with the toils of the day:
  When it said the word “GUILTY!” the Jury all groaned,
  [aki 2.5zw]And some of them fainted away.

@style verse
  Then the Snark pronounced sentence, the Judge being quite
  [aki 2.5zw]Too nervous to utter a word:
  When it rose to its feet, there was silence like night,
  [aki 2.5zw]And the fall of a pin might be heard.

@style verse
  “Transportation for life” was the sentence it gave,
  [aki 2.5zw]“And [italic: then] to be fined forty pound.”
  [aki 0.5zw]The Jury all cheered, though the Judge said he feared
  [aki 2.5zw]That the phrase was not legally sound.

@style verse
  But their wild exultation was suddenly checked
  [aki 2.5zw]When the jailer informed them, with tears,
  Such a sentence would have not the slightest effect,
  [aki 2.5zw]As the pig had been dead for some years.

@style verse
  The Judge left the Court, looking deeply disgusted:
  [aki 2.5zw]But the Snark, though a little aghast,
  As the lawyer to whom the defense was entrusted,
  [aki 2.5zw]Went bellowing on to the last.

@style verse
  Thus the Barrister dreamed, while the bellowing seemed
  [aki 2.5zw]To grow every moment more clear:
  Till he woke to the knell of a furious bell,
  [aki 2.5zw]Which the Bellman rang close at his ear.

